      "text": "To determine the area of figure \\(ABCD\\), we need to analyze the given sectors and how they combine. The figure \\(ABCD\\) consists of two sectors of a circle with a radius of 12, each having a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\).\n\nFirst, let's calculate the area of one sector. The formula for the area of a sector of a circle is given by:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of a sector} = \\frac{\\theta}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi r^2\n\\]\nwhere \\(\\theta\\) is the central angle in degrees and \\(r\\) is the radius of the circle. For our sectors, \\(\\theta = 60^\\circ\\) and \\(r = 12\\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\\[\n\\text{Area of one sector} = \\frac{60^\\circ}{360^\\circ} \\times \\pi \\times 12^2 = \\frac{1}{6} \\times \\pi \\times 144 = 24\\pi\n\\]\nSince there are two such sectors, the total area of these two sectors is:\n\\[\n\\text{Total area of two sectors} = 2 \\times 24\\pi = 48\\pi\n\\]\nTherefore, the area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is \\(\\boxed{48\\pi}\\).",
