      "text": "To determine the area of the figure \\(ABCD\\), we need to analyze the given information and the geometry of the problem. The figure \\(ABCD\\) consists of two sectors of a circle of radius 12, each with a central angle of \\(60^\\circ\\).\n\nFirst, let's calculate the area of one sector. The area \\(A\\) of a sector of a circle with radius \\(r\\) and central angle \\(\\theta\\) (in radians) is given by the formula:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{1}{2} r^2 \\theta\n\\]\nHere, the radius \\(r = 12\\) and the central angle \\(\\theta = 60^\\circ\\). We need to convert the angle to radians:\n\\[\n60^\\circ = \\frac{60 \\pi}{180} = \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\text{ radians}\n\\]\nNow, substitute \\(r = 12\\) and \\(\\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{3}\\) into the formula:\n\\[\nA = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 12^2 \\times \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 144 \\times \\frac{\\pi}{3} = \\frac{144\\pi}{6} = 24\\pi\n\\]\nSince there are two such sectors, the total area of the two sectors is:\n\\[\n2 \\times 24\\pi = 48\\pi\n\\]\nThus, the area of figure \\(ABCD\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{48\\pi}\n\\]",
